course: biology
lesson: 169
title: The Arctic Ocean: walruses, bowheads, the 400-year shark and the unicorn's tooth
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Arctic Ocean lies around the North Pole under a lid of floating sea ice that grows in winter and shrinks in summer, and the animals there live on, under and around that lid.
explain: A walrus climbing out onto the ice is like you hauling yourself out of a swimming pool with two ice picks: it hooks its long tusks into the ice and drags its heavy body up.
explain: Under the ice swim some of the oldest animals on Earth: bowhead whales that may live over two hundred years and Greenland sharks that may live about four hundred, so a shark alive today could have been born before your great-great-great-great-grandparents.
explain: Long ago traders sold narwhal tusks in Europe as unicorn horns; the tusk is really a long spiral tooth, and as the world warms the sea ice is shrinking, making life harder for polar bears and seals.
fact: A walrus has two long tusks, and walruses use their tusks like ice picks to pull their heavy bodies out of the water onto the ice.
fact: Walruses find clams on the dark seabed with hundreds of sensitive whiskers and suck them out of their shells.
fact: Bowhead whales can smash through ice with their heads to make breathing holes, and they may live for over two hundred years; old harpoon tips from the 1800s have been found in living whales.
fact: The Greenland shark is the longest-lived animal with a backbone known, perhaps living about four hundred years, and scientists measured its age using a kind of carbon clock in the lens of its eye.
fact: Belugas are white whales called sea canaries because they whistle, click and chirp, and a beluga can change the shape of its forehead, called the melon, to aim its clicks.
fact: Polar bears hunt seals by waiting at breathing holes for hours or even days.
fact: Polar bear skin is black and its fur is made of see-through hollow hairs that look white because they scatter light.
fact: Ringed seals dig snow caves on the ice to hide their pups and keep breathing holes open by scratching the ice with strong claws.
fact: Arctic sea ice is shrinking because the world is getting warmer, and some polar bears now have to swim much longer distances, sometimes for days.
fact: Even in the dark polar night some tiny sea animals move up and down in the water, which surprised scientists who thought life there almost stopped in winter.
fact: Harp seal pups are born white on the ice, turn grey after a few weeks, and grow very fast on milk that is about half fat, building a thick layer of fat called blubber before they must swim in icy water.
fact: In the Middle Ages narwhal tusks were sold in Europe as unicorn horns; a narwhal's tusk is a tooth that grows out through its lip in a spiral, mostly in males.
fact: The arctic tern makes the longest migration of any animal, flying from the Arctic to Antarctica and back each year, and in a lifetime it may fly as far as to the Moon and back several times.
exercise: How does a walrus use its tusks to get out of the water? | Like ice picks to pull itself onto the ice | To dig a staircase of little steps into the ice | To spear fish to eat on the ice | It never leaves the water at all
exercise: How does a walrus find clams on the dark seabed? | With hundreds of sensitive whiskers | With a glowing light on its head | By listening to their songs | With its tusks like a metal detector
exercise: How does a bowhead whale make a breathing hole in thick ice? | It smashes through with its head | It melts the ice with warm breath | It bites the ice with its big teeth | It waits for a polar bear to dig it
exercise: What was found in some living bowhead whales? | Old harpoon tips from the 1800s | Messages in old glass bottles from sailors | Gold coins from a sunken ship | Mammoth bones
exercise: About how long may a Greenland shark live? | About four hundred years | About forty years at most | Only about four or five years | About four thousand years
exercise: How did scientists measure a Greenland shark's age? | With a carbon clock in the lens of its eye | By counting the rings on its teeth, like a tree | By asking old fishermen who saw it born | By weighing it
exercise: Why are belugas called sea canaries? | They whistle, click and chirp | They are bright yellow like canaries | They fly over the waves | They live in cages
exercise: What does a beluga change the shape of to aim its clicks? | Its forehead, called the melon | Its long tail | Its front flippers | Its blowhole, which squeaks like a whistle
exercise: How does a polar bear catch seals? | It waits at breathing holes for hours or days | It chases them across the open sea at top speed | It digs them out of the seabed | It calls them like a beluga
exercise: What colour is a polar bear's skin? | Black | White | Pink | Yellow
exercise: Why does a polar bear's fur look white? | The see-through hollow hairs scatter light | The hairs are full of white paint | Snow sticks to every single hair all the time | The hairs are made of ice
exercise: Where do ringed seals hide their pups? | In snow caves on the ice | In nests high up in trees | In burrows under the water | On the backs of big whales
exercise: How do ringed seals keep breathing holes open? | By scratching the ice with strong claws | By breathing hot air on them for hours | By biting the ice | By asking the walruses to help them dig
exercise: Why is Arctic sea ice shrinking? | The world is getting warmer | Polar bears are eating it | Ships are carrying it away to warmer seas | The Moon pulls it south
exercise: What colour is a harp seal pup when it is born? | White | Black | Grey | Brown with spots
exercise: How much fat is in harp seal milk? | About half | Almost none | About one part in a hundred | It is all fat and no water
exercise: What were narwhal tusks sold as in the Middle Ages? | Unicorn horns | Dragon's teeth | Elephant tusks | Walking sticks for kings
exercise: What is a narwhal's tusk? | A long spiral tooth that grows through its lip | A horn growing from its forehead like a rhino's | A sharp fin on its back | A bone from its nose
test: «got a unicorn horn in grandpa's attic, worth millions» What is it most likely? | A narwhal tusk, which is really a whale's tooth | A real unicorn's horn | A goat's horn painted white | A walrus whisker
test: «polar bears are white all the way through, duh» What is right? | Their skin is black and their hairs are see-through | Yes, white skin and white hair | Their skin is white and their hairs are black underneath | They are grey under the fur
test: «3 walruses on the ice, how many tusks» | 6 | 3 | 9 | 12
test: «2 narwhal boys, one tusk each, n a walrus, how many tusks altogether» | 4 | 3 | 6 | 2
test: What would happen to polar bears if the sea ice melted much earlier each year? | They would have less time to hunt seals from the ice and swim longer | They would get fatter | They would move to Antarctica and hunt penguins instead of seals | Nothing at all
test: «that shark's 400?? no way, they're just guessing» What is right? | Scientists measured it with a carbon clock in its eye lens, though the age is only an estimate | They counted its birthdays | It is a guess with no evidence | Sharks live only ten years
test: In a cartoon a beluga talks in words like a person. What is real? | Real belugas whistle, click and chirp but do not speak words | Belugas speak English | Belugas make no sounds at all, they are the silent whales | Belugas only bark like dogs
test: «a whale with a harpoon from like 1880 in it, it's a fake story» What is right? | It is true: bowheads can live over two hundred years | It is fake, because whales only ever live about twenty years | The harpoon was put in last year | It was a shark, not a whale
test: «the arctic's dead in winter, nothing moves in the dark» What did scientists find? | Tiny sea animals still move up and down in the dark water | Everything freezes solid | Only polar bears are awake, and every sea animal sleeps until spring | The sea turns to rock
test: A bird ringed in Greenland is seen next summer near Antarctica. Which bird is most likely? | An arctic tern | A white snowy owl | A ptarmigan of the tundra | A penguin flying north
test: Why does a harp seal pup need milk that is about half fat? | To grow fast and build blubber before it must swim in icy water | To make its fur white | To stop it from growing too big for the den | Because seals cannot drink water
test: «why do walruses have whiskers, they're not cats lol» What is right? | They feel for clams on the dark seabed | To look like cats | To keep their noses warm in the freezing water | To scare off polar bears
exam: Identify the principal method by which the age of the Greenland shark has been estimated. | The analysis of a radiocarbon record contained within the lens of the eye | The enumeration of the rings within its teeth | The measurement of its weight | The testimony of fishermen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colouration of the polar bear. | The skin is black and the translucent hollow hairs appear white by scattering light | The skin and hairs alike are pigmented white | The skin is white and the hairs are pigmented black | The animal is grey beneath a layer of snow
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called unicorn horns traded in medieval Europe? | They were in fact the spiral tusks of the narwhal, which are teeth | They were the horns of a now extinct horse of the northern forests | They were the antlers of reindeer | They were fashioned from walrus whiskers
exam: Ascertain the principal consequence for the polar bear of the progressive diminution of Arctic sea ice. | A reduction in the time available for hunting seals from the ice and longer distances to be swum | An increase in body weight | A migration of the species to Antarctica | The adoption of a diet of plants exclusively
